id that they  failed to keep pace with the changing  nature of this crime  so the inquiry report focuses heavily on  the fact that so much and exploitation  of children  now occurs through the internet um it  referenced a lot particularly in  relation to um boys being um exploited  and things like the dating app grinder  and  things like that as being um ways of  grooming  and sucking children into this  exploitation and effectively that local  authorities and police forces are just  struggling to keep up with the pace of  how the criminals are  using technology to change their methods  of exploitation  so then how do you go about unpicking  this and dealing with this issue  i think we need a complete reset on  child sexual exploitation in this  country i think that um some brilliant  work was done um in the past and coming  out of and the horrors of rochdale and  rotherham it really felt like there was  a few years ago a real renewed energy  both within the government at that time  and certain police forces and local  authorities to tackle this issue and i  feel that that energy is all fizzled  away and we need a complete reset we  need to be completely honest that this  problem hasn't gone away it's not  getting less it's getting  getting worse that it's everywhere and  that we need to actually have a  completely new um strategy at a national  level to tackle child sexual  exploitation and that has to be fed  through into the number of prosecutions  so one of the inquiry recommendations is  about sentencing that um should be an  aggravating factor and added to the  sentencing act and 2020 if somebody's  convicted of offences relating to the  sexual exploitation of children that's  brilliant but we know that so many cases  don't even get to be prosecuted let  alone convicted because we have systemic  issues with the resourcing of our  criminal justice system so the police  force is investigating these crimes and  the cps deciding to prosecute these  crimes just isn